\margin{\uline{NB} We use $y\in\mathrm{Cof}$ $\longleftarrow$ here}
We use the weak lifting \unsure{lemma|lemma} for
\diagram{\begin{tikzcd}
\varnothing \arrow[r] \arrow[d] & x \arrow[d,"f","\sim"'] \arrow[r,dashed,hook,"i","\sim"'] & \bar x \arrow[dl,dashed,"\alpha\ \deleted{\unsure{\varphi}}","\sim"'] \\
y \arrow[r,"1"] & y \arrow[ur,dashed,bend right=45,"g"'] & {}
\end{tikzcd}}
so there exist $i,\alpha$ with $\alpha i=f$, \deleted{\unsure{and}} $i\in W\cap\mathrm{cof}$, and $\alpha$ admits a section $g$
\[\alpha g=1_y\]
I say that $(g,i)$ is the inverse \inserted{sought.} \deleted{$\unsure{\alpha g=\operatorname{id}_y}$}
\deleted{Let us then consider}

For $(gf,i)\sim\uline{1}_x$, we would like to use the commutative diagram alongside, the only trouble is that, we have $\alpha\in W$ but not nec. $\alpha\in\mathrm{cof}$, so $\alpha i\ (=f)$ not nec. in \deleted{\unsure{W}} $\mathrm{cof}$ (whereas it is in $W$).
\diagram{\begin{tikzcd}[column sep=large,row sep=small]
x \arrow[dd,"1_x"'] \arrow[dr,"gf"] \arrow[dddrr,dashed,"f" near end] & & x \arrow[dl,"i","\sim"'] \arrow[ddll,"1_x","\sim"'] \\
&\bar x \arrow[ddr,dashed,"\alpha","\sim"']&\\
x \arrow[drr,dashed,"f"'] & \deleted{\unsure{f}} &\\
&& y
\end{tikzcd}}
This would work if we could find $y\xrightarrow[\sim]{\beta}y'$ \inserted{in $W$,} such that the composite $\beta f$ is not only in $W$, but moreover, in $\mathrm{cof}$.

Clearly, this is not \unsure{alw.} the case (take the case where the $\mathrm{cof}$ are the monos, and where $f$ is \uline{not} a mono !). This looks bad !
\diagram{\begin{tikzcd}
x \arrow[d,"f"'] \arrow[dr] &\\
y \arrow[r,"\beta"']&y'
\end{tikzcd}}
We shall therefore instead first define the canonical functor
\[\widetilde C\longrightarrow W^{-1}C\]
\[\unsure{\Phi|\varphi}:\quad x\longmapsto x\]
\[(f,i)\longmapsto[i]^{-1}[f]\]
it is immediate that this is compatible with identities and composition. So we do have functor \deleted{\unsure{injective}} \inserted{bijective} on objects. I say that it is surjective on arrows; It suffices to see, by composition, that if $f:x\longrightarrow y$ is in $W$, then $[f]^{-1}$ comes from $\widetilde C$. We shall take \deleted{$(y,x)$} $y\longrightarrow x$ in $\widetilde C$, defined by $(g,i)$, \deleted{\unsure{A priori that}} $[i]^{-1}[g]$, to prove that $[i]^{-1}[g]=[f]^{-1}$, it suffices to prove that $[f][i]^{-1}[g]=[\uline{1}_y]$, now $\alpha i=f$ implies $[\alpha][i]=[f]$ so $[f][i]^{-1}=[\alpha]$, so the \unsure{product} is $[\alpha][g]=[\alpha g]=[1_y]$, OK.
